is  gray area so we tend to look at these  great speeches that Abraham Lincoln  gives and say you know these are very  profound discussions that he's having  and that's true but then he's operating  in the middle and he's giving himself a  lot of leeway one way or the other and  he's looking to see how this is gonna  kind of work work its way out and of  course when when he were you when he ran  for reen amah nation in 1864 he thought  he was going to lose and so everything  that he'd been working for all these  years and his prosecution of the war he  thought was a complete failure and then  things started to turn around on the  battlefield and that's really where this  war was run was won or lost and the  Democrats started to take power again in  late 1862 early 1863 robert e lee is  starting to move his way north to make  his final stand at gettysburg in july  and that's where it falls apart for the  democrats  their success or failure no matter how  much political gain they're making is  really being determined on the  battlefield by the Confederate Army  you mentioned that you and your  co-author kind of disagreed on whether  or not link was right and kind of how he  handled things were there any other  issues that you had in researching or  writing this book you know with two  different personalities I guess trying  to write a book together I mean is there  anything else that came up well one of  the interesting things about the book  was how much research has changed during  the course of a 10-year project when we  started of course it was all time spent  at local historical societies at the  Library of Congress and that's one of  the interesting things about this book  is most of the time was spent at these  small historical societies reading  newspapers that nobody's touched in a  hundred and forty years so we wanted to  tell this new story from their own  perspective when we started it was all  done on site and now you're able to do a  lot of your preliminary research on the  Internet of course you're reading full  text of books you can do keyword  searching on the Abraham Lincoln papers  at the Library of Congress so that  changed a lot as far as just the  research side of it and I think one of  our benefits is that we looked at things  very different jeff is more politically  inclined and he likes to look at the  political advantages and disadvantages  of the situation where I look more I  think at the social side and I want to  know what John Hatchin was thinking I  want to get inside this guy's head and  find out what's motivating him is it is  it his religion his dad whether his  father was a Methodist preacher and what  effect did that have on his views how  did that affect his views on slavery  Jeff would look at more of the political  leanings of that and I'm interested in  the social side I want to know how this  affected his family life I want to know  how this affected his relationship with  his son who tried to be a little more  moderate at the beginning of the war and  pulled the paper away from this kind of  violent tempered John Hutchins editorial  and so there was differences as far as  that perspective goes but again I think  that makes the book stronger because you  do get all those points of view  when I was reading Lincoln's wrath I  guess I was a bit shocked because I  didn't realize how cunning and brilliant  he was especially when it came to  politics and you call them one of the  great media presidents thinking about  presidential studies and even the  president today or in the next  administration how often do you think  administration's look at Lincoln in this  side of his story to maybe think about  what's most important civil liberties or  the nation's security do you think that  this has an impact I think it absolutely  has an impact and I think I think all  presidents look to Abraham Lincoln for  guidance on civil liberties for guidance  on on how to how to get the support of  the people remember Lincoln went into  office with the lowest popular vote of  any US president so this is a guy who's  coming into office and he wasn't  everybody's hero he wasn't the savior of  the United States most people didn't  want him there and I think for  presidents to look at how to rally the  troops sort of say how to get public  opinion in your favor Lincoln didn't  have public opinion polls officially but  that's what the press was during that  time and Lincoln was a master  manipulator of the press he was not this  kind of oh gee shucks lawyer from  downstate Illinois who happened to be  elected president of the United States  he was writing anonymous editorials in  newspapers hey in Nicolay when he was in  office is writing anonymous editorials  editorials under pseudonyms in papers  throughout the North trying to get his  message out to the people before he's  elected Lincoln buys a German language  speaking newspaper in Springfield to  pass his message along to  german-speaking voters and when he's  elected president he quietly sells the  paper because that voting block is  important to him and he realized that's  how he's going to get his message across  so he did these sorts of things he's a  he's a master politician he's not a back  woodsman by this time he knows what he  wants  and he knows how to get it and he's  playing by the same rules that all the  other politicians are playing by so this  really shouldn't be a surprise  to anybody when we look at how complex  of a figure Abraham Lincoln was he was  just better at it I think is what it  boils down to in the end any other  questions anything on your mind okay  well thank you very much again for  coming
 

